Total yearly cost = Initial cost + Maintenance cost + Cost of lemons = \boxed{100.5}

Now, let's find out the year when the lemon tree will start earning money by comparing the profit earned with the yearly cost:

Annual Net Income = Sale price of lemons - Cost of lemons - Maintenance cost = (7 * $1.5) - $10.5 - $3 = $5.5

In the year when the net income becomes positive, the tree starts earning money. To find the number of years required, we can divide the total initial cost by the annual net income:

Number of Years = Initial cost / Annual Net Income = \boxed{90 / 5.5}

Number of Years = \boxed{16.36}

Since trees cannot be planted in fractions of years, it will take 17 years before the lemon tree starts earning money.

So, the answer is \boxed{17}.

